Exactly — **O(n)**.

Your reason is the important part: inserting at the front changes the position of every existing element, so the amount of work grows with the list size.

Compare these two:

```python
nums.append(50)      # add at the end
nums.insert(0, 50)   # add at the front
```

`append(50)` is usually **O(1) amortized** because Python can place the new value at the end without shifting the existing elements.

`insert(0, 50)` is **O(n)** because the existing elements must shift right.

Now try the reverse operation:

```python
nums = [10, 20, 30, 40]
nums.pop(0)
```

Would removing the first element be **O(1)** or **O(n)**? Why?